[Learn From My Mistake: Do not try to continue your ride / adventure with too short a chain, as it is too easy to break your derailleur hanger when shifting](https://www.reddit.com/r/bicycling/comments/bfszn1/learn_from_my_mistake_do_not_try_to_continue_your/)

 My perspective comes from an equivalent department store bike my partner bought (onsale,  at LL Bean's) when the kids were small, so that she could keep up with them on short rides.  That bike eventually morphed into a commuter.  It's on it's 3rd bottom bracket, and is approaching end of life after 13 years and about 25,000 miles

&amp;#x200B;

Neither is a bad choice. Both will fit your need, both will require you to have some mechanic support to keep them running.   When new, the Giant was likely slightly higher quality, but it's hard to know how it's been used over the years.  The Giant has the advantage of coming with rack and water bottle holder, which will save you $25 or so.

&amp;#x200B;

If the Giant is in good condition, I'd lean that way
